From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 13:20:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] llama: LLAMA_PLE_PREFETCH=1 reads the PLE rows of a batch's
 later ubatches in the background

Qwen3.8-Flash-Next hashes each token's n-gram (3 tokens, 16 heads) to rows of a 28.8 GB
IQ4_NL table (per_layer_token_embd: 320,001,536 rows of 90 bytes, shard 2 of the GGUF),
lazily mapped (--lazy-mode). A ubatch's rows are random pages of that file, cold for any
text not seen before, and the CPU gather runs before the ubatch's graph, serially, with
the GPU idle. Measured offline (no GPU, no stack; random rows of the shard on this
machine, 8 threads on E-cores, n=1 each): 8,192 rows (one 512-token ubatch) cold in
~230 ms by the mmap path llama.cpp uses (35,900 rows/s), the same at 16 threads
(35,200), and ~240 ms by 4 KiB-aligned unbuffered positional reads in file order (the
mechanism of upstream PR #29030 and of Strata's 5b7e316) -- so the device's random-read
rate is the limit, not the number of readers or the I/O path. A 30K-token prompt is 480K
rows: ~14 s if all are cold.

Strata's 5b7e316 (C-4: the embedding in one gather, the n-gram table read by several
threads) does not apply as it stands: llama.cpp already gathers a whole ubatch's rows in
one GET_ROWS, in parallel over rows. What can be won here is overlap: the rows of
ubatch k+1 are known while ubatch k computes. This reads them then. It is ours, not a
Strata port.

llama_ple_prefetch (llama-ple-prefetch.cpp): one worker thread per context; each decode
call submits its batch's tokens past the first n_ubatch (the first ubatch's own gather
is in the way), the worker computes their rows (llama_ple_rows, the arithmetic of
set_input() moved out so both use it; 32,000,000 random rows compared against the old
inline code, no mismatch) and touches the first and last byte of each distinct row,
ubatch by ubatch in the order the decode reaches them, in file order inside one, on 8
threads (LLAMA_PLE_PREFETCH_THREADS; or LLAMA_PLE_PREFETCH=N), stopping when a newer
batch arrives. It reads and discards: nothing it does can change a result. One sequence
and tokens only (an embeddings batch, or several sequences, is skipped); a batch of one
ubatch has nothing to hide behind and is skipped. Off unless LLAMA_PLE_PREFETCH is set;
LLAMA_PLE_PREFETCH_LOG=1 prints one line per batch. With the table not host-mapped (no
--lazy-mode) nothing is created.

What it hides: with -b 2048 -ub 512 three of a batch's four ubatches' gathers (the
first is not). NOT MEASURED in the engine: the offline rate says up to ~0.23 s a
512-token ubatch when the rows are cold, against ~2 s a ubatch now (PCIe-bound expert
copies) but a few tenths once those are fixed; how many of a real prompt's rows are
cold (repeated trigrams are served from the page cache) is not known. Compiled and linked
with the series; not run on a GPU.
